														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4602256">
									

<p>This started off well enough (the sticks detail was&#8230; strange in a fun way), but by the end there was really just&#8230; way, too much text for the story you're telling. This is especially a problem in the last 3 logs, which were basically &quot;We're going to try this thing.&quot; &lt;Thing works exactly as planned.&gt;, no interesting or unexpected results. When your story runs out, don't make me keep reading.</p>
<p>For an example of what to do different, you don't need to have him making a speech about how much effort he put into the video. Just describe an overwrought video. That would give you an opportunity to put in more of the fun otherworld-ness.</p>
<p>(Too much text was an issue even before that. I don't need lengthy &quot;realistic&quot; decommissioning proposal documents. The point of &quot;realistic&quot; documentation is verisimilitude, and after that has been achieved it rapidly wears out its welcome.)</p>
<p>(Personal bias, but I guess it also kinda hurts that the description of the copyright scene really accurately gave me the impression of&#8230; let's say a character I'm unlikely to empathize with. That's just a minor point, though.)</p>

							</div>

								
